NOTICE
======

aquaskk-rust is a Rust reimplementation (port) of the AquaSKK input
method engine:

    AquaSKK — MacOS X implementation of the SKK input method
    https://github.com/codefirst/aquaskk
    Copyright (C) 2002-2004 phonohawk
    Copyright (C) 2005-2013 Tomotaka SUWA <tomotaka.suwa@gmail.com>
    and the AquaSKK contributors (see the upstream AUTHORS file)
    License: GNU General Public License v2 or later

The engine architecture, state machine, editors, dictionary handling,
keymap format, configuration files (kana-rule.conf, keymap.conf and the
other files under data/), and the acceptance test data
(crates/aquaskk-core/testdata/session_test.dat) are ports of, or taken
from, the upstream AquaSKK sources. This project is distributed under
the same license (see LICENSE).

The macOS input method under macos/ is a new Swift implementation, but
it was written with reference to the upstream Objective-C++ sources
(platform/mac): the NSEvent-to-engine-event translation follows
SKKPreProcessor.mm, the controller structure follows
SKKInputController.mm, and the bundle configuration follows the
upstream Info.plist.

Portions derived from third-party work bundled with AquaSKK:

  * jconv — Japanese character code conversion library
    Copyright (c) 2006 Tomotaka SUWA
    Portions copyright (c) 2000-2003 Shiro Kawai (from Gauche)
    BSD-style license. The EUC-JP codec tables in
    crates/aquaskk-core/src/jconv/eucjp_tables.rs are generated from
    the EUC-JISX0213 (JIS X 0213:2000 Appendix 3) vs Unicode mapping
    table: Copyright (C) 2001 earthian@tama.or.jp, All Rights Reserved;
    Copyright (C) 2001 I'O, All Rights Reserved
    ("You can use, modify, distribute this table freely.")

  * GenericStateMachine — Generic State Machine Library for C++
    Copyright (c) 2006-2008 Tomotaka SUWA
    BSD-style license. Ported to Rust as
    crates/aquaskk-core/src/machine.rs.

  * The SKK dictionary format and the SKK input method itself originate
    from SKK (Simple Kana to Kanji conversion program) by Masahiko Sato
    and the SKK Development Team (https://skk-dev.github.io/dict/).

